WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:06.449
Here, we see an example of an intrusion detection system I DS alert.

2
00:00:06.809 --> 00:00:10.840
This is being provided as an example of how threats are identified.

3
00:00:11.199 --> 00:00:15.130
Some of the concepts in this scenario are more advanced than this course.

4
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.639
So don't be alarmed if you don't understand everything discussed here,

5
00:00:19.549 --> 00:00:23.950
we'll start by reviewing the main points of the data that is presented to us.

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.680
Note that in this example,

7
00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:30.159
the host name and user name fields have been removed to maintain anonymity.

8
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:37.099
This tells us that the I DS detected the use of software called advanced IP scanner

9
00:00:37.229 --> 00:00:42.099
that can be used by Attackers to enumerate or look through the network scanning

10
00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.540
addresses to see what services are running on the computers in the local network.

11
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.849
This software is also used by network or system

12
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:54.099
administrators to inventory a local network for troubleshooting purposes.

13
00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:55.240
Finally,

14
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.709
this top section of the alert screen tells us

15
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.409
that the event was reported by an endpoint agent,

16
00:01:00.590 --> 00:01:06.400
meaning that it was generated by a host intrusion detection system, H I DS solution,

17
00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:09.000
not a network intrusion detection system

18
00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.379
N I DS.

19
00:01:11.129 --> 00:01:13.760
This line identifies the host that is running

20
00:01:13.769 --> 00:01:16.309
the suspicious process as a Windows system.

21
00:01:17.389 --> 00:01:22.769
This process section identifies the start time process name and ID or P

22
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.050
ID number that correlates to the process in the Windows task manager.

23
00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:29.949
This can be helpful in a couple of ways.

24
00:01:29.959 --> 00:01:33.730
First, the start time tells us how long the process has been running.

25
00:01:34.059 --> 00:01:37.129
The P ID can also give some clues as lower P

26
00:01:37.239 --> 00:01:40.629
ID numbers may indicate a process that started running during the boot

27
00:01:40.639 --> 00:01:44.830
sequence and higher numbers indicate something that was started much later.

28
00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:49.370
These lines give us the details of the executable file,

29
00:01:49.379 --> 00:01:52.750
including the path to the file itself as well as the

30
00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.139
actual command line that was used to run the executable.

31
00:01:56.489 --> 00:01:59.879
These are important contextually as they show the program

32
00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:03.269
executed from a temp folder under the user's id,

33
00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:07.839
which typically does not require administrative privileges in a windows system.

34
00:02:08.350 --> 00:02:11.410
In other words, they could be run by any average user.

35
00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:15.720
The command line used shows additional context including that

36
00:02:15.729 --> 00:02:18.699
the application is running as a portable application,

37
00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:23.279
meaning that it doesn't have to be formally installed on the machine to execute.

38
00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:25.520
In this case,

39
00:02:25.529 --> 00:02:28.080
there is not enough context to really know if

40
00:02:28.089 --> 00:02:30.940
this process is being used in a malicious manner.

41
00:02:31.399 --> 00:02:35.940
Like many security alerts, this one relies on some human interaction.

42
00:02:36.169 --> 00:02:39.509
So you should contact the end user assigned to this asset

43
00:02:39.619 --> 00:02:43.339
to inquire whether they are in fact running this software.

44
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.149
And if they have a legitimate business reason to do so,

45
00:02:46.669 --> 00:02:48.929
if you discover that this was intended.

46
00:02:48.979 --> 00:02:51.289
It might be a good place to explain too that

47
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.690
you were alerted because this legitimate software can be used

48
00:02:54.699 --> 00:02:58.089
by threat actors to conduct reconnaissance on the local network

49
00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:01.169
to determine where there might be weaknesses to exploit.